{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e22f4082-815d-4291-8ec0-471a24337109","agentId":"ab1d0f0cc6658635f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Build the file `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/cg_contraction.py` — the Clebsch-Gordan contraction route for computing the projected cubic P_N[|ψ|²ψ] in coefficient space, WITHOUT going through pointwise synthesis/analysis.\n\nContext: This is part of the M8.5-C2 qualification protocol. § 4.3 requires two independent routes for computing the projected cubic nonlinearity that must agree to rounding. Route 1 (transform route) is in `build/galerkin.py` and goes synthesis → pointwise cubic → analysis. Route 2 (this one) must compute the same thing purely in coefficient space via CG contraction.\n\nThe scientific object: ψ is a section of a vector bundle over S³ = SU(2), valued in W_ρ (an irrep of 2I), expanded in an equivariant Galerkin basis. The cubic is ⟨ψ,ψ⟩ψ where ⟨·,·⟩ is the Hermitian inner product on W_ρ (giving a scalar |ψ|² times the W_ρ-valued ψ).\n\nFor the R0 sector (trivial representation, d_ρ=1), ψ is scalar-valued and the basis functions are just the S³ harmonics (the unitarized symmetric powers π_n). The cubic ⟨ψ,ψ⟩ψ = |ψ|²ψ.\n\nIn coefficient space, if ψ = Σ c_α f_α where f_α are orthonormal basis functions, then the Galerkin coefficients of |ψ|²ψ are:\n\n(P_N[|ψ|²ψ])_α = Σ_{β,γ,δ} c̄_β c_γ c_δ ∫_{S³} f̄_α f̄_β f_γ f_δ dμ\n\nThe coupling integral ∫ f̄_α f̄_β f_γ f_δ dμ can be computed via CG coefficients. The key identity: the product of two representations decomposes via CG coefficients, so π_n ⊗ π_m = Σ_l CG_{n,m}^l ⊗ π_l ⊗ CG_{n,m}^{l,T}.\n\nCRITICAL: Do NOT materialize the full rank-4 coupling tensor (it would be M^4 entries where M~1000). Instead, compute per-level contractions.\n\nFor the scalar (R0) sector, each basis function at level n is f_{n,i,j}(x) = A_{n,i} π_n(x) e_j where A is an intertwiner (d_ρ × (n+1)). For R0, d_ρ=1 so A is 1×(n+1) and the function is scalar.\n\nThe CG approach for the cubic:\n1. The product ψ̄·ψ at level (n1,n2) involves π_{n1}^* ⊗ π_{n2}, which decomposes as Σ_l π_l with CG coefficients.\n2. Then multiplying by ψ at level n3 involves another CG decomposition.\n3. Project onto level n4 using orthogonality.\n\nThe SU(2) CG coefficients for Sym^{n1} ⊗ Sym^{n2} → Sym^l are the standard ones: the tensor product of spin j1=n1/2 and j2=n2/2 contains spins |j1-j2| to j1+j2.\n\nUse the existing code in `build/group.py` which has:\n- `pi_n_unitary(n, q)`: unitarized Sym^n \n- `multiplicity(rho, n)`: multiplicity of irrep rho in level n\n- `_sym_gram(n)`: Gram matrix entries k!(n-k)!/n!\n\nAnd `build/sections.py` which has:\n- `build_basis_object(rho, N)`: returns dict n -> (mult, d_rho, n+1) arrays\n- `total_modes(rho, N)`: total Galerkin modes\n\nAnd `build/quadrature.py` which has the synthesis/analysis transforms.\n\nThe function signature should be:\n```python\ndef project_cubic_cg(coeffs, basis_obj, N, rho='R0'):\n    \"\"\"Compute P_N[|ψ|²ψ] via CG contraction in coefficient space.\"\"\"\n```\n\nFor numerical stability at high n, use the CG recurrence approach.\n\nThe key mathematical fact for the CG contraction: for scalar functions on S³,\n∫_{S³} π_{n1}(x)_{a1,b1}^* π_{n2}(x)_{a2,b2} π_{n3}(x)_{a3,b3} π_{n4}(x)_{a4,b4}^* dx\nfactors via CG coefficients of SU(2).\n\nActually, for the SCALAR cubic on S³ with the orthonormal basis, the coupling integral simplifies because the basis functions have explicit forms in terms of the unitarized symmetric powers and the intertwiners.\n\nFor the implementation, use the quadrature-verified approach:\n1. Compute the coupling integrals level-by-level using CG decomposition\n2. For each pair of output level (n_out) and input levels (n1, n2, n3), compute the coupling via intermediate CG decomposition\n\nA simpler but equivalent approach: compute the Galerkin matrix elements by building them level-by-level using the Peter-Weyl orthogonality and CG coefficients. The orthogonality of the matrix elements of irreducible representations gives:\n\n∫ D^{n1}_{a1,b1}^* D^{n2}_{a2,b2} dx = δ_{n1,n2} δ_{a1,a2} δ_{b1,b2} / (n1+1)\n\nFor the cubic (degree 4 in the matrix elements), we need triple products, which involve 3j symbols.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach: use the fact that the SU(2) CG decomposition lets us compute the coupling coefficients. For the product ψ̄ψ (which involves π_n^* ⊗ π_m, i.e., conjugating one factor), the CG decomposition gives intermediate levels |n-m| ≤ l ≤ n+m. Then for the second multiplication by ψ at level n3, we get another CG decomposition.\n\nBuild a helper that computes the SU(2) Clebsch-Gordan coefficients ⟨j1,m1; j2,m2 | J,M⟩ for given spins. Then use them to contract level by level.\n\nThe CG coefficients for SU(2) can be computed via the standard recurrence or Racah formula. Since we're working with Sym^n (spin n/2), the CG coefficients for the Kronecker product decomposition are well known.\n\nTest the implementation by comparing against the transform route at a small cutoff (N=4 or N=8) on random coefficient vectors. They must agree to machine epsilon.\n\nIMPORTANT: The protocol says \"Neither route materializes the rank-4 coupling tensor (at M ≈ 1000 retained modes it exceeds 10^12 entries); coupling is applied via transforms or via per-level CG contraction only.\"\n\nWrite the file, then test it by running a comparison with the transform route at N=4 for R0.\n\nRead these files first to understand the existing code:\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/group.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/sections.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/quadrature.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/galerkin.py`"},"uuid":"d9b47c1f-1d94-4146-a4a2-196ee9a6d876","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:38:38.023Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"d9b47c1f-1d94-4146-a4a2-196ee9a6d876","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ab1d0f0cc6658635f","attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_delta","addedNames":["EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","Monitor","NotebookEdit","SendMessage","TaskStop","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__complete_authentication"],"addedLines":["EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","Monitor","NotebookEdit","SendMessage","TaskStop","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__complete_authentication"],"removedNames":[],"wireHiddenNames":[],"readdedNames":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"1fb54573-c822-4cac-8eca-8a46b2572a44","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:38:38.036Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"1fb54573-c822-4cac-8eca-8a46b2572a44","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ab1d0f0cc6658635f","attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- design: Create a design canvas - a multi-artboard visual design published as an Artifact that runs Claude Design's canvas editor (an early preview of Claude Design inside Claude Code). You DRAFT the design as .dc.html artboards laid out on one pan/zoom canvas; where saving is enabled for the user's account they refine every element visually (click-to-select, a properties panel, inline text editing, undo/redo) and Save publishes a new version for everyone, otherwise they get a view-and-export (PNG/PDF) preview of your draft. Good for UI mockups and screen flows, landing pages, marketing and social graphics, and print pieces - posters, flyers, brochures as single-page artboards; memos and reports as one flowing artboard. Use when someone wants a design, mockup, wireframe, UI or screen design, landing page, poster, flyer, brochure, banner, card, one-pager, or any visual layout they would rather tweak by hand than in code. Only for CREATING or re-seeding a canvas; an existing one is edited in its published Artifact.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, or handing the viewer a file to save. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever the user asks for an artifact needing any such runtime behavior.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings; ultra: deep multi-agent review in the cloud); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review. For ultra on a GitHub.com PR target, --post asks to post the finished review’s findings to the PR as a single comment from the user’s GitHub account (not a review; the launch dialog still confirms in interactive sessions, while non-interactive mode posts on the flag alone) and --no-post hides that option.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init\n- security-review","skillCount":18,"isInitial":true,"names":["design","dataviz","artifact-design","artifact-diagramming","artifact-capabilities","update-config","keybindings-help","code-review","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","schedule","claude-api","workflow-authoring","claude-in-chrome","run","init","security-review"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7eed2f40-47d1-49f3-b338-40802ddeed8d","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:38:38.036Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
